WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Five bottles on the prep room bench have

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:03.240
lost their labels.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:08.160
The list says they hold cyclohexane, cyclohexene, ethanol,

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.200
2-methylpropan-2-ol and ethanoic acid.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.560
They're all colourless liquids, so looking won't help.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.560
You can't see a double bond or an

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.720
O H group, but you can see what

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:19.680
it does.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.720
Three quick tests, each watching for one group,

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.160
can rule candidates out.

00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:28.166
Each test borrows a reaction from Module 7.

00:00:28.647 --> 00:00:30.566
The trick is to pick a reaction that

00:00:30.647 --> 00:00:33.126
only one of these groups does quickly, with

00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:35.366
a change you can see: a colour that

00:00:35.447 --> 00:00:38.166
goes, a colour that changes, or a gas

00:00:38.247 --> 00:00:39.166
that fizzes.

00:00:39.447 --> 00:00:41.606
So before you test, predict.

00:00:42.087 --> 00:00:43.846
Which group adds bromine?

00:00:44.087 --> 00:00:45.926
Which group is acidic enough to react with

00:00:46.007 --> 00:00:46.726
a carbonate?

00:00:47.127 --> 00:00:49.126
And which group can be oxidised?

00:00:50.334 --> 00:00:53.093
First, the carbon to carbon double bond.

00:00:53.374 --> 00:00:55.533
Add a few drops of orange bromine water

00:00:55.694 --> 00:00:56.653
and shake.

00:00:56.974 --> 00:00:59.293
Bromine adds across the double bond and gets

00:00:59.374 --> 00:01:02.013
used up, so the orange colour fades within

00:01:02.174 --> 00:01:02.733
seconds.

00:01:03.294 --> 00:01:04.973
You ran this test in the bromine water

00:01:05.054 --> 00:01:05.613
practical.

00:01:06.254 --> 00:01:08.813
An alkane has no double bond to open,

00:01:08.894 --> 00:01:10.013
so it stays orange.

00:01:10.574 --> 00:01:14.413
On our list, cyclohexene fades it and cyclohexane

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:15.293
doesn't.

00:01:15.614 --> 00:01:18.493
Bright light lets an alkane react slowly, so

00:01:18.574 --> 00:01:20.653
test in normal room light and read it

00:01:20.734 --> 00:01:21.453
at once.

00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:24.586
Next, the carboxylic acid.

00:01:24.747 --> 00:01:27.066
Add sodium hydrogen carbonate.

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:30.266
A carboxylic acid is a stronger acid than

00:01:30.347 --> 00:01:33.386
carbonic acid, so it gives the hydrogen carbonate

00:01:33.467 --> 00:01:34.426
a proton.

00:01:34.747 --> 00:01:37.066
The carbonic acid breaks up into water and

00:01:37.147 --> 00:01:40.106
carbon dioxide, and the tube fizzes.

00:01:40.227 --> 00:01:42.466
Bubble that gas through limewater, and it turns

00:01:42.507 --> 00:01:45.466
milky, which confirms carbon dioxide.

00:01:45.547 --> 00:01:47.626
An alcohol is far too weak an acid

00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:49.866
to do this, so it doesn't fizz.

00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:52.706
Then the hydroxyl group.

00:01:52.987 --> 00:01:56.426
Warm the sample with acidified potassium dichromate.

00:01:56.507 --> 00:01:59.786
A primary or secondary alcohol is oxidised, and

00:01:59.867 --> 00:02:02.666
the orange dichromate ions are reduced to green

00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:04.746
chromium three ions.

00:02:04.827 --> 00:02:07.546
Ethanol is a primary alcohol, so it turns

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:08.586
green.

00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:11.306
A tertiary alcohol has no hydrogen on its

00:02:11.387 --> 00:02:14.346
O H carbon, so it stays orange.

00:02:14.427 --> 00:02:17.226
Alkanes and ethanoic acid usually leave it orange

00:02:17.307 --> 00:02:18.266
too.

00:02:18.307 --> 00:02:21.626
Esterification is a second hydroxyl test, but it

00:02:21.707 --> 00:02:25.146
relies on judging a smell, so here dichromate

00:02:25.227 --> 00:02:25.946
does the work.

00:02:27.167 --> 00:02:29.166
Green tells you something in the tube was

00:02:29.247 --> 00:02:30.206
oxidised.

00:02:30.447 --> 00:02:33.086
Aldehydes do that too, so green points to

00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:36.526
a primary or secondary alcohol only when aldehydes

00:02:36.607 --> 00:02:38.286
aren't among the candidates.

00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:40.366
On our list, they aren't.

00:02:40.447 --> 00:02:43.166
An alkene might also turn it green, so

00:02:43.247 --> 00:02:46.206
after a bromine fade, green alone can't prove

00:02:46.287 --> 00:02:47.646
an O H group.

00:02:47.727 --> 00:02:49.806
And orange doesn't prove there's no O H

00:02:49.967 --> 00:02:50.526
group.

00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:52.926
A tertiary alcohol has one, and stays orange

00:02:53.007 --> 00:02:54.046
anyway.

00:02:54.167 --> 00:02:57.166
So orange means no primary or secondary alcohol

00:02:57.247 --> 00:02:58.126
was detected.

00:02:59.334 --> 00:03:01.333
Use a fresh portion of the sample for

00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:04.453
each test, so one reagent can't spoil the

00:03:04.574 --> 00:03:05.453
next result.

00:03:05.734 --> 00:03:08.373
Then the order doesn't change any result.

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:11.013
It changes how much hazard you face.

00:03:11.174 --> 00:03:15.093
Start with the mildest reagent, sodium hydrogen carbonate,

00:03:15.174 --> 00:03:17.013
then bromine water.

00:03:17.054 --> 00:03:20.933
Dichromate contains chromium six, so it comes last,

00:03:20.974 --> 00:03:23.253
as a teacher demonstration in a fume cupboard.

00:03:24.467 --> 00:03:25.426
Here's the trap.

00:03:25.827 --> 00:03:28.386
A yes for one group doesn't mean no

00:03:28.467 --> 00:03:29.466
for the others.

00:03:29.747 --> 00:03:32.466
Prop-2-en-1-ol has a double bond and an O

00:03:32.627 --> 00:03:34.786
H group, as its structure shows.

00:03:35.027 --> 00:03:37.426
It fades bromine water, so it looks like

00:03:37.507 --> 00:03:38.146
an alkene.

00:03:38.627 --> 00:03:41.346
It also turns dichromate green, which fits its

00:03:41.427 --> 00:03:43.746
alcohol group, though the double bond might add

00:03:43.787 --> 00:03:44.226
to that.

00:03:44.787 --> 00:03:46.946
If you stopped at the first yes, you'd

00:03:47.067 --> 00:03:48.306
never look for the alcohol.

00:03:48.867 --> 00:03:50.679
In the same way, an acid with a

00:03:50.707 --> 00:03:52.546
double bond would fizz and fade.

00:03:52.947 --> 00:03:55.506
So run all three tests, and report each

00:03:55.587 --> 00:03:56.786
group on its own.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.240
Back to the bench.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.560
One bottle gives no fizz, so ethanoic acid

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:03.600
is out.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.600
Bromine water stays orange, so cyclohexene is out.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:12.000
Dichromate turns green, so cyclohexane and the tertiary

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.720
alcohol are out, because both would stay orange.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.120
Only ethanol fits all three results.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.680
Notice that the no results did half the

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:23.680
work, so write down every result, even when

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:24.920
nothing happens.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.440
Every no rules a candidate out.

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:31.306
Suppose another bottle had given no change in

00:04:31.387 --> 00:04:32.706
all three tests.

00:04:32.987 --> 00:04:37.386
That fits cyclohexane, and it fits 2-methylpropan-2-ol, so

00:04:37.507 --> 00:04:39.506
these tests can't separate them.

00:04:39.787 --> 00:04:42.746
You'd need different evidence, like an infrared spectrum,

00:04:42.827 --> 00:04:44.346
which shows an O H band.

00:04:44.987 --> 00:04:46.986
They also can't say whether an alcohol is

00:04:47.067 --> 00:04:49.706
primary or secondary, or where a double bond

00:04:49.787 --> 00:04:50.466
sits.

00:04:50.747 --> 00:04:52.986
They show which groups are present, not the

00:04:53.067 --> 00:04:53.946
whole structure.

00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:56.506
For that, you'll use the spectra coming up

00:04:56.547 --> 00:04:57.466
in this module.

00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:02.186
One line for the three tests: fizz, fade,

00:05:02.427 --> 00:05:02.746
green.

00:05:03.307 --> 00:05:05.906
Fizz for a carboxylic acid, fade for a

00:05:05.947 --> 00:05:08.826
double bond, green for a primary or secondary

00:05:08.907 --> 00:05:09.466
alcohol.

00:05:10.027 --> 00:05:12.666
Run all three, each on a fresh portion.

00:05:13.867 --> 00:05:14.506
Your turn.

00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:17.386
An unknown is one of the four compounds

00:05:17.467 --> 00:05:18.506
on the card.

00:05:18.587 --> 00:05:21.786
It gives no fizz with sodium hydrogen carbonate,

00:05:21.867 --> 00:05:25.626
it decolourises bromine water within seconds, and it

00:05:25.707 --> 00:05:28.106
turns acidified dichromate green.

00:05:28.667 --> 00:05:31.466
Identify the unknown, and justify your answer.

00:05:32.027 --> 00:05:33.386
Pause here to answer.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.320
No fizz rules out hexanoic acid.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.840
The fading bromine water points to a double

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:52.520
bond, which rules out hexane and hexan-1-ol.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:56.280
Only hex-5-en-1-ol is left, and its green dichromate

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:58.040
result fits its O H group.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:01.880
Picking hexan-1-ol because the dichromate went green is

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:04.680
the slip: every result has to fit.

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:06.540
Last one.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:10.300
Liquid Q is an alkane, an alkene, an

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:12.940
alcohol or a carboxylic acid.

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:16.540
It gives no fizz with sodium hydrogen carbonate,

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:18.700
and a student concludes that Q is an

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:19.420
alcohol.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:21.420
Evaluate this conclusion.

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:25.180
Then outline two further tests, saying what each

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.500
result would support, and one limit.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:29.260
Pause here to answer.

00:06:39.667 --> 00:06:41.866
The conclusion isn't supported.

00:06:42.147 --> 00:06:45.346
No fizz only rules out a carboxylic acid,

00:06:45.427 --> 00:06:47.746
so Q could still be an alkane, an

00:06:47.827 --> 00:06:49.786
alkene or an alcohol.

00:06:50.067 --> 00:06:52.786
On a fresh portion, a bromine fade supports

00:06:52.827 --> 00:06:53.946
a double bond.

00:06:54.227 --> 00:06:57.186
Warm another with dichromate: if the bromine stayed

00:06:57.347 --> 00:07:00.866
orange, green supports a primary or secondary alcohol.

00:07:01.427 --> 00:07:04.786
One limit: a tertiary alcohol would stay orange.

00:07:05.027 --> 00:07:07.266
Treating one no as a yes for something

00:07:07.347 --> 00:07:08.786
else is the trap.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:12.800
So, when you meet an unknown, ask three

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:13.760
questions.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.200
Does it fizz, does bromine water fade, does

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:18.960
dichromate turn green?

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:21.760
Answer each on a fresh portion, let every

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:24.720
no rule candidates out, and report each group

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:26.400
the results support.

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:30.080
Next, the practical: running these tests safely, with

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:31.360
the right controls.

00:07:32.567 --> 00:07:34.006
Here are the key notes.

00:07:34.247 --> 00:07:36.166
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:36.247 --> 00:07:37.046
to copy them.
